From: Bogdan Mircea <mirceapetrebogdan@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: avoid backlog requeue when GRO is disabled

DSA receives packets in the network receive context, after the conduit
has already applied RPS. When GRO is disabled on a user port,
gro_cells_receive() calls netif_rx(), queuing the packet to a backlog a
second time even when the user port has no RPS map. This adds queue
admission and locking to every forwarded packet.

Use netif_receive_skb() directly for an active user port that cannot use
GRO. It retains user-port RPS and generic XDP processing. Keep the
transport-header reset performed by gro_cells_receive(), and leave
inactive ports and traffic eligible for GRO on the existing path. DSA
switch timestamp deferral remains before this handoff; the direct receive
path also performs the normal generic PHY timestamp check.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Bogdan Mircea <mirceapetrebogdan@gmail.com>
---

--- a/net/dsa/tag.c
+++ b/net/dsa/tag.c
@@ -120,7 +120,16 @@ static int dsa_switch_rcv(struct sk_buff *
 	if (dsa_skb_defer_rx_timestamp(p, skb))
 		return 0;
 
-	gro_cells_receive(&p->gcells, skb);
+	if (netif_elide_gro(skb->dev) && netif_running(skb->dev) &&
+	    (skb->dev->flags & IFF_UP)) {
+		/* Already in the receive context; retain user-port RPS and XDP
+		 * without an unconditional second backlog admission.
+		 */
+		skb_unset_transport_header(skb);
+		netif_receive_skb(skb);
+	} else {
+		gro_cells_receive(&p->gcells, skb);
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
